leeroth@my-deja.com (Lee Roth) wrote in message news:<d0e744c9.0202252211.260a9f7c@posting.google.com>...

> The DEC TU45 (shudder, curse) and the TU78/TU79 were built by Pertec.

Boy did I mess that up!

The TU45 was built by Pertec and was a big piece of crap- unreliable,
poor design, etc. Tape speed was 75 ips (I think), density 800/1600
bpi.

The TU77 was also built by Pertec and had the same densities as the
TU45 but was better designed, more reliable and had a higher tape
speed (125 ips).

The TU78 (Pertec) was same tape speed at the TU77 but had
1600/6250 density.

The *TU79* was a real drive sold by DEC, but predates all of the
above- it was sold by (probably) CSS (computer special systems) arm of
DEC and was simply an HP-built 'dancing arm' tape drive that was *very
reliable*. This drive was made available so that BOC (Bell operating
companies) could use them in packaged configurations, chiefly AMARC
which was a PDP11/40 based system that took toll call records from
many different toll switches and wrote the data to magtape.

I found a very good info about the TU45/TU77/TU78 and their massbus
controllers (and our friend the TS11) at
http://www.inwap.com/pdp10/usenet/tu45

The above URL mentions that TU45/77/78 were 'collaboratve' efforts
between DEC and Pertec.

It amazes me how well I knew this stuff years ago and have forgotten
so much of it now- sigh.

Lee Roth
 
