sword7@speakeasy.org wrote:

> Also, according to NetBSD/vax sources, I noticed that DEQNA is similar
> like DELQA.  What is difference between DEQNA and DELQA?  To
> detect DELQA by write 1 to interrupt vector register to find out.  If 
> zero, then it is DEQNA.


According to the DELQA manual, to distinguish
between a DEQNA and a DLEQA in DEQNA-lock mode,
do the following:

1. Write VAR<00> = 1
2. Immediately read  VAR
    If VAR<00>=1 this is a DELQA
    If VAR<00>=0 this is a DEQNA
3. Write VAR<00> = 0

The text implies that you should actually
start with VAR<00>=0 for the test to
be valid (and it should normally
be set that way anyway).

So it sounds like NetBSD is doing the right thing.

Antonio




