sword7@speakeasy.org writes:
>I am surprised to notice that Ultrix products are not covered by
>Hobbyist license.  Why??

DEC's use of Ultrix came out of the old BSD 4.2 and AT&T USL licenses.  I'm
sure that there are lots of complex and hard to understand cross licensing
issues between Dec, Berkeley, and USL.  It was nice to see DEC put out some of
the old code for the hobbyist licenses, but it is probably asking a lot to
have Compaq pay for lawyers to dig through the mess to figure out if its OK
for a hobbyist binary of Ultrix.  Ultrix was/is not pure DEC.

BTW its been awhile but when I was doing Ultrix the Decnet was an end-node
only implementation and may have been phase-IV only as well.  I'm not sure
if the Decnet for Ultrix was ever enhanced beyond end-node/phase-IV.  What
the Ultrix Decnet _was_ good for was a bridge between a Decnet network and
a TCP/IP network.  It came with some tools to be a Decnet/TCP gateway.

Later

Mark Hittinger
bugs@pu.net


