On 25 Feb 2002, Wolfgang Rupp wrote:

> I have no idea about RedHat for Alpha. SuSE comes with a program
> named "swriteboot". You need this to write the boot loader onto the
> first few blocks of the disk. AND you must leave those few blocks
> blank when you partition the disk, first hand. I.e., partition c
> is the whole disk and starts at 0, but a must start later, or you
> will not have space for the boot loader (bootlx in SuSE).

 Better yet create a small boot partition at the beginning (if you have a
label entry to spare).  This way you won't forget about it.  Also you need
not create a "c" partition spanning the whole disk -- Linux doesn't need
it and actually doesn't use it.  A separate device is provided for a whole
disk access regardless. 

 As to DEC 3000/300X -- someone with such a system has to fill holes in
the code first to make Linux running on it.  It shouldn't be that
difficult as docs are available and both Alpha and TURBOchannel are
supported by Linux.  So it's only board support to be completed.

