On Fri, 22 Feb 2002, Brian Inglis wrote:

> On Fri, 15 Feb 2002 13:30:26 -0000, sword7@speakeasy.org wrote:
> 
> >Hello folks:
> >
> >I am looking for DHQ-11, DHU-11, and/or DHV-11 programming specifications
> >for terminal ports.  Does anyone have a machine-readable copy of them?
> >
> >I only have DZ11/DZQ11 programming specs that do not have many features
> >of DHx-11 series.
> 
> I don't know if it's included with the DH[QUV]-11 specs, but to
> get modem control and hangup capability, you needed to add the
> DM-11-BB Modem Control Multiplexor and cut some jumpers on the
> DH-11. Without the DM-11-BB, all terminals were considered local
> and always connected: you couldn't tell when the user hung up and
> you couldn't disconnect the user. 

The DM11 is a Unibus modem control unit used with the DH11 (and DV11
and maybe some other multiplexers).  It was not used with the
DHV/DHQ/DHU or other members of that family.  

The DH11 is a very early (1972) Unibus 16-line mux, made mostly or
entirely from 74-series TTL.  It required a 9-slot system unit (2
quad slots dedicated to the DM11, which was optional IIRC.)

The DHV11 is a dual or quad (don't remember - I could take apart
my MicroVAX and find out...) Q-bus 8-line mux.  The DHU11 is a
similar hex Unibus 16-line mux, introduced at about the same time,
in the mid-1980's.  They were similar enough that they could use
the same drivers, but were totally different from the DH11.
I think the DHQ11 was a dual Q-bus successor to the DHV11, looked
pretty much the same to software.  Later, there were CXY08 (8-line,
with modem control) and CXA16 (16-line, data leads only) Q-bus
cards in the S-box form factor.  They were used in DECServer 500/550
terminal servers and in many later MicroVAX.  (Three CXY08's came
with our VAX 4000-200.)  They are either identical or extremely
similar to the DHV/DHQ.

The DH11 did DMA output and had an input silo, which reduced
interrupts and allowed it to function correctly even if all
interrupts didn't get serviced immediately.

The DHU/DHV/DHQ also did DMA output, a much bigger input silo,
and could handle XON/XOFF flow control in hardware.

