There might be more than one memory cards in the computer.

When you open the "backside" of the computer box, the CPU-card is the
top-most card. After it, there is one or more memory-cards, and after that
there are I/O-communications cards (like serial interafce, disk interface
etc)

You can find (search google) "field service guide" that is a conversion
table from the memory module number Mxxxx-AA to a model name/type, where
you also can se how large each memory module is.

You can replace with other modules, other sizes etc rather freely! Each
memory module has a "strap field" or switch of some kind, where you/the
service engineer installs the base address of that module. The module then
addresses its memory strictly increasing from this address. Information of
how to address the various modules is available for most modules at varous
corners of the Internet.

Example: One system with first memory module 128 kW large, second module
256 kW large and third module 64 kW large (a total of  448 kW =3D 896 kByte=
)
would have its first card addressed at 0 (zero), second card addressed at
128 kW, third card addressed at 128+256=3D384 kW.

Using this, and the faulty address given in the diagonse program, you can
decide which memory card is defective, if there are more than one in the
computer.

If you can get access to proper drawings/information on this very module,
then the information on which bit that is defective and at which address
is enough information to decide exactely which memory device is faulty,
thus it is possible to just exchange that very integrated circuit (beware,
it must not be the memory device that is faulty, it is also possible that
some kind of driver/reciever for internal signals on the memory card might
be broken giving the same result to the outside, or in worst case, a wire
on the card might be broken or shortcut.

In other words, there is some work for either a field service engineer (to
replace the faulty module and send it for repair) or for a skilled
electrical engineer/Master of science in electrical engieering-
microcomputer engineering to find and repair the very problem.

Take care with regards of ESD when working with these cards/modules so
that you dont accidentily break any more module while working on the
machiene!

God luck with the project

/G=F6ran =C5hling

