> Interesting to reflect on the comparative values of the equipment each
> company sold, and the relative amount of research that each undertook.
> 
> Reminds me a lot of the book written by Harvard's Michael Porter,
> 'Competitive Strategy'.
> 
> Comapq pursued the 'low-value high volume' model, whereas Digital followed
> the 'low-volume high value model'.

In the early 60's, DEC *was* the "low-value high volume" model.
They sold pre-stuffed PCB's that the user could plug together
into his own system, and then started selling "Programmed Data
Processors" rather than "Computers" because they were targeting
a market that didn't have the purchasing authority to buy a
"Computer".

Things, of course, are relative, and both the world and DEC
had greatly changed by the late 80's when the DEC sales force
was ignoring PO's from anyone not high enough on the Fortune 500.

Tim.


