This has worked for me in the past.
Make a UFS filesystem on an 'a' partition of the disk, do not make
it larger than the capacity of your cd-rom. Make it bootable (read the
man page for disklabel).
Copy the files you want onto the disk, ensure you have the osfboot files
and a kernel, the easiest way to do this is to copy a Tru64 CD and work
from there. Use vdump or dd to copy this.
When you have a hard disk which will boot and do what you want, dd out
an image of it and burn that onto cd.
Adam





