Actually, we fell because we failed to make the right strategic decisions.
When John Sculley came around, we should have aligned ourselves with Apple
for the desktop and tossed the PC out the door. The Alpha would have been a
good chip under the direction of Digital, Apple and Motorola. Our business
models were very much aligned in that we designed most if not all of the
product ourselves and manufactured almost all of the product ourselves, ie;
Hardware and Software. Anyone who has used or still has copies of DECwrite,
DECpresent, DECcalc, etc. knows only too well that this software was
eveloving to a point that exceeded Microsofts capabilities.

The moment the first PC snuck in the back door it should have met it's maker
in the crusher.

Bob Palmer should never have been left in charge of the company, he never
knew how unique and strong the technology and capabilities were within the
various engineering groups. He sold off all the jewels.

When the various industries started talking about core competencies and
ditching anything that wasn't, we should not have listened to them.
Everthing we did at Digital was a core competency. We created it and
evoleved it and we mae it. We were years ahead of the competition in the
application of technology. Today, our disk technology and tape technology
lives on in a competitors product. Intel wouldn'y be enjoying such a strong
technology curve if it weren't for what they copied and then later acquired
or were given.

We dipped our toes in many waters, and just when the waves started splashing
ashore we pulled out, with fright. Fault Tolerance (A design second to
none), Printers (We could have whooped those HP guys), Scanners (We never
got serious, but should have), Infoserver (Network storage for everyone and
every OS, people still want this product and it hasn't been made in years).

Then there was the relationship with Olliveti. A nigthmare at best,
substandard material, the workforce used to call in sick when these units
were scheduled for build. The metal stamped in Italy was in fact stretched
and shredded so every edge was a knife and whenever we built these things,
the employee base had their hands cut up all the time. Then there was the
Laptop and the Portables from Olliveti. Probably close to 200,000 were
recalled and crushed and a subsequent contract given to RadioShack in a
desperate effort to recover market share.

Basically, if we had aligned with Apple we'd have been OK, if we had toughed
it out like Apple we'd have been OK.

Now, the new guy wants to sell the company to HP, seems like Bob Palmer is
still steering the ship.

Oh, why did Compaq fail to absorb DIGITAL? Because they never integrated the
technology groups, 3 different part number systems, three different
engineering philosophies, three different ECO philosophies, in fact, too
many three different everything, one from Compaq, one from Tandem and one
from Digital.

Compaq is a firm that typically throws the towel in on something and then
wonders why the hell they did that, without proving first that al
alternative even existed let alone worked.

rtt

I could go on, but why bother, it's a dead horse. If I thought that anyone
could learn a lesson from this, I'd write a book, but I have discovered that
since World War II, the concept of good mangement is a a falicy. If the
management of today were managing the war effort against the Nazi's, we'd be
loosing real bad.

I look at a firm like Business Depot or Staples, and I see that one of their
core competencies is management. Does that surprise anyone. Their management
from the top down, train the employee base. They don't hire some outside
consulting firm to train their staff. Why, because no outside firm knows
what their business is, or their service philopsophy. These are things only
senior management can pass on to junior management through training.

Digital used to be very strong on training, but we got out of that, it
wasn't considered a core competency. Poppycock.
Digital got out of writing database software, it wasn't considered a core
competency. Poppycock. (Oracle)
Digital got out of writing Software Applications, it wasn't considered a
core competency. Poppycock, what did we use to run our business? Did we not
sell this stuff to others so they could run succesful businesses too?
Digital got out of disk drives. Quantum is very successful with having
acquired Digital technology.
Digital got out of tape drives. Again, DLT, a very strong technology.


What went wrong you say, too many things, with no real visionary at the
helm, with no real marketing team working the charm, with no real picture of
what we wanted the future to look like.

Go get em' Bill. More power to you.




