RD53's (Micropolis 1325 IIRC) have been known to lock up after very long
periods of non operation. What actually happens is that the heads stick
to the platter and this results in too much friction for the drive motor
to spin the platters up. This can sometimes be fixed by rocking the
drive gently side to side in the vertical axis. If this fails, carefully
remove the screws holding the drive electronics board, lift the board
and _very_  gently, nudge the motor shaft back and forth until it
rotates freely. To reformat the drives, you need the xxdp utilities -
maybe someone here has the relevant utility. 

The Connor scsi drives used on early Macs were famous for the sticking
head problem, but were often fixable well enough to recover the data
using the above hack.

Chris


