None of the DHx11 interfaces do DMA on receive. When these interfaces
were designed the normal input stream was a person typing at a keyboard.
The overhead required to set up single character DMA makes that method
too expensive, compared to the SILO technique.

The main difference between the DHU11 and the QBUS DHV11 (besides the
number of ports 8-) is the DHV11's lack of a SILO timeout timer. The
DHU11 will buffer input characters in it's SILO until 1) the SILO is
75% full, or 2) after a programmable amount of time has elapsed. The
DHV11 will interrupt after the SILO goes from empty to not-empty.

The latter means that each of the eight inputs can generate a single
interrupt. The DHU11, on the other hand, will wait (say 25 MS) after
the SILO goes not-empty before generating the receive interrupt. One
or more of it's other 15 input ports may also have loaded the SILO
before the timer expires. Thus, only a single interrupt is needed for
multiple input characters. The faster the per line baud rates the
better this method becomes.

The DHQ11 replacement for the DHV11 adds the SILO timeout timer. On a
real (8-) QBUS PDP-11/VAX the DHQ11 is to be preferred over the DHV11.

Jeff Campbell
n8wxs@arrl.net

 

