Well, if you go back far enough there was: The 'original' DECtape
(TU56) with the fat (0.75 inch wide?) little reels was pretty good...
it had redundantly written data tracks and addressable blocks. It was
the 'floppy disk' of its time... I think some of the operating systems
could actually use the DECtape as the 'system device'.

I worked on a lot of PDP11 and PDP15 systems that had these DECtapes
on them and other than cleaning heads or replacing light bulbs, I
don't think I *ever* pulled a single service call on one of them- rock
solid and reliable.

Legend has it that you could demo the DECtape reliability by taking a
paper punch and randomly punching a few holes at random down the tape
and it would still read just fine.

Talk about flashbacks: Check out http://www.pdp8.net/tu56/tu56.shtml -
you can watch the TU56 in action via your RealPlayer! Oh the (core)
memories!

Lee Roth


