Tim,

Jeff presented an excellent comparison of the DHU, DHV and DHQ options. 
Also popular during the era were DZ(Q) and DMF-32.  [I don't have all of
these devices or manuals for them any more; just going on fuzzy memory.]

My opinion from that time was that the DZ(Q) was never well liked.  It
offered no DMA on output.  Thus it was interrupt driven on both input and
output.  That hurt on interactive -11s, but was murder on VAXs of the time
(780, uVax, etc.).

The DMF-32, if I recall correctly, introduced with the uVAX II(?), offered
8 async serial ports, 1 parallel line printer (like) port, and 1 sync
serial port.  If you're looking for faster uploading devices or
networking-like devices, this might be the one for you.  The async ports
operated similar to the DH, I believe.  The sync port could run DDCMP and
thus be used for DECnet (or other nefarious purposes).

Good luck,

John

