This matches my 1981 VAX Arch Handbook...  Are you setting a temp
value to the max (requested mode, previous mode) or are you setting
the mode arg of the PROBE instruction?  If you change the instruction's
arg, I can easily see things breaking.  (For example, same code is checking
accessibility of a buffer from the caller's mode.  Sometime the caller
is user mode (prev_mode=3) and sometimes it's kernel (prev_mode=0), so
the 1st call from user mode modifies the arg to a "3" and it stays
there forever, thus producing spurious errors when prev_mode is kernel.

The first edition (what year? mine doesn't say what edition it is, just
"1981" on the spine) doesn't have the self-relative queue instructions,
and who knows what else.  I can't imaging VMS working with out them,
though.

The current Macro-32 assembler manual (VAX Macro and Instuction Set
Reference Manual) is included in the online DOCS and should be at
the Compaq doc site:

  http://www.openvms.compaq.com:8000/
