Brian,

First, no point in installing P-II 400's.  They won't run at 400MHz, because the
LX chipset supports only 66MHz FSB.

Second, the same point holds true about ANY Pentium III's.

Third, your best option may be a pair of PPGA Celeron 533's with a pair of cheap
but SMP-capable slotkets.  The other option could be faster FC-PGA Celerons with
66MHz FSB, coupled with a slotket capable of SMP AND voltage regulation.  The
FC-PGA Celerys run a 1.7v, 1.65v, 1.6v, all well below the 2.0v CPU voltage
supported by your motherboard.

All this said and done, you might find that SMP Celerys do not work, or even
that a Celery will not work at all.  I'd take it carefully with minimum
investment and try a single PPGA Celery 533 with SMP-capable slotket.  If that
goes well, install another one.

Unfortunately, not too many companies or individuals have experimented with the
ex-DEC Pentium II boxes, because there weren't built in great quantity like
Dell, Gateway, etc... Ben Myers

