section).  I successfully booted KA655 firmware to the >>> prompt but
I only have a problem with test 53 (countdown 31).  It complaint that 
programmable timers are slightly too fast or slow (subtest 9 and A).
Test 53 tested programmable timers how fast or slow by using TODR 
register.  I am figuring out why because my clock service calibrates two 
timers by using instructions/sec to update tick rate each 10 msecs (each 
tick is estimated 1 kHz).

On NetBSD/vax 1.5.2, I successfully booted system without any problems.
NetBSD boot reported that MicroVAX 3800/3900 series instead of MicroVAX II.
I was able logged into system.

On OpenVMS v7.2, it resulted the same like MicroVAX II! It stalled after
2nd RQDX3 initialization (with interrupt enables).  Well, I made a log 
from Simh VAX emulator to compare with my debug.log.  Yes, same addresses 
and contents on both log files.  (On MicroVAX II, there were very 
different addresses and contents.)  I now detected some differences 
between my debug.log and sim_log files during RQDX3 initialization.  I 
suspect perhaps queue instructions.  First queue has same address but 
2nd, 3rd, and 4rd queues had different addresss (difference is hex 200).

When OpenVMS detected Unit #1 has medium online, it did requested read 
access (21) on 1st queue.  OpenVMS started to send online and 2nd gus 
commands to the unit #1 (boot drive).  When it received 2nd GUS reponse
from RQDX3 (Unit #1), it processed and labeled as SABKUP$DUA1 but removed 
a read access request!  That cause the OpenVMS system to stall.  It never 
happened in Simh VAX emulator.   If it does not remove a read access
request, it will continue to read some blocks and display time/data dialog 
later.

