Assuming ring transition interrupts are enabled...

The command ring transition interrupt should occur only when the command
ring is full - the port owns all the slots - and the port has removed
(processed) one of the entries. The interrupt tells the host that
another
command may be issued, assuming a) there is another command and b) there
are enough credits to allow the command to be posted to the port.

A ring sized at 32 slots needs to have 32 MSCP commands pending
before the port will generate the command ring transition interrupt. It
will not generate an interrupt when removing one command from the ring
if ring did not also have 31 other commands pending.

A "ring" sized with 1 slot should always generate a transition
interrupt as each posted command is processed.

The logic is: the ring has gone from full to not full.

The receive transition interrupt scheme works the other way: an
interrupt will be generated when the receive ring goes from empty
to not empty. That is, the port has posted a response to the host.

The command and receive rings are circular FIFO queues. Your emulation
code needs to keep track of where the next expected command will be
and where the next response needs to go. This is done by honoring
the ownership bit in each ring entry. After initialization, the host
owns all the command ring entries: the ownership bit in each of the
command ring entries is a zero. The port owns all the response ring
entries: the ownership bit in each entry is a one.

