Jeff:

Yes, that is right.  That's why OpenVMS stalled after RQDX3 initialization 
and all units scanned.  When RQDX3 initialization was completed, OpenVMS 
checked saved interrupt flags from ring base table and see command 
interrupt flag is set then jumped to check all queues by using 
INSQUE/REMQUE instructions but found nothing.  Then returned back to
null job and never do the read access.

After it was fixed in my RQDX3 emulation, now all saved interrupt flags
are response interrupt is one but command interrupt is zero.  OpenVMS
checked everything before exit RQDX3 initization.  When it made sure that
command interrupt is zero, it gave green signal to start I/O data acceses.  
Then OpenVMS issued IPL 8 software interrupt and returned back to null 
job.  Then interrupt immediately occured after REI executed and checked 
any requests.  It found something and issued IPL 4 software interrupt and 
returned back to null job.  Then interrupt immediated occured and started 
to execute at IPL 4.  It jumped into the I/O data routines and tried to 
dequeue I/O requests from its queue (80AE6350) at 80C68AD9.  It found 
queue empty and gave up.  It just gave up before next instructions that 
is starting I/O data access.  It retuned back to null job forever....

According to debug.log, it failed to enqueue I/O requests to 80AE6350
during MSCP initialization step #1.  It did in SIMH emulator.  That
is big difference that I detected in both log files.  In TS10 emulator,
it never encounter 80C7476D that contains INSQUE instruction with queue 
80AE6350.  In SIMH emulator, it did encountered 80C7476D.  There are
300,000 lines between step #1 and step #2.  According to VAX
Internal and Data Structures, it is in processing at step 44 during
EXE$INIT routine.  I believed that LDR$CQ_IMAGES (sp?) perhaps is messed 
up or so.

-- Tim Stark

