<eric-no-spam-for-me@brouhaha.com> wrote:

>The KL10-A is only used in the 1080, was in tall cabinets, and has a
>KL10-PA, though it could be field upgraded to a -PV or -PW.  It did not
>have internal RH20 channels.

>In summary, when someone says "Model A" or "Model B", it is ambiguous.
>But it's most likely that they are referring to the KL10-PA or KL10-PV,
>and not the system-level packaging.  A 2040 (KL10-C) was often configured
>with a KL10-PA, in which case it can be considered to have a "Model A"
>backplane.

So therefore the 2040s with a KL10-PV (model B) could be more easily
upgraded to a 2060 than a 2040 with KL10-PA (model A). As you wouldn't
have to replace the model A CPU? Which is what I thought I recalled
anyway. 

A 1080 initially fitted with a KL10-PA if field upgraded to a KL10-PV
FOC (high level of system crashes and more convenient for DEC to
replace with a model B than a model A) by DEC would, in deed, have
reduced the number of spares F-S had to hold onto if all other KL-10s
locally were model B. Again that fits in with what I recall happening.

Machines in question were #1115 (1080 Dundee University) and #2172
(2040 upgraded to 2060) Dundee College of Technology. The replaced
KL10-PA from #1115 was on display in Dundee University Computer Centre
(DEC didn't want it back) for many years and may even still be there.

