The Australian Computer Museum Society (WA) is a group of enthusiastic
individuals dedicated to preserving Western Australia's computer heritage.
The ultimate aim of the Society is to set up a museum to display the
computers used in the development of the state. A not inconsiderable sum of
money and countless hours of time have been expended in collecting and
storing the present collection which presently fills a 200 square metre
purpose built barn. Some highlights of the collection include WA's first
commercially installed machine, a first generation Bendix G-15 used by the
Main Roads Department for engineering calculations relating to the Mitchell
Freeway. Other machines include an IBM 360 mainframe and many DEC machines
from the late 60's onwards.

The Society is seeking interested individuals to assist in the development
of the musuem. Computer literacy is definitely not a requirement as there
are many tasks not involving the collection, such as locating suitable
premises, fund raising, etc. While all skills are important, some the
Society
could use immediately are hardware and software skills in relation to DEC
machines. Many need to be recommissioned, and sorting the significant items
from the insignificant will greatly reduce the pressure on storage space.
The Society would especially like to hear from anyone with librarian or
museum skills.

To find out more about the Society and the people behind it, an invitation
is extended to two events. The first event is to be held on the afternoon of
Sunday 17 Febuary in Mundaring. The second event will be held on the
afternoon of Sunday 24 Febuary in Dianella. For further information, email
lextron@iinet.net.au

Lex Cunningham
President
Australian Computer Museum Society (WA) Inc

PS: Membership of the Society is prohibitively expensive at $10 a year
concession, and $25 a year for the rest :)










